    14	# Design analysis for the combo variant
    15	# a) Concrete behavior and trace:
    16	#    - We keep the original visible objects from the spec: a square background, several 2x2 monochrome
    17	#      blocks, and one shared 4x4 frame color around each block. The combo changes only what happens
    18	#      after emission.
    19	#    - To make "pass through and exchange color" readable on a single-layer pixel raster, the generator
    20	#      uses one corner gap per frame, so every beam is diagonal. A diagonal-vs-diagonal crossing is the
    21	#      one place where two trajectories can visibly pass through each other without fighting for one cell.
    22	#    - Small trace: a red NE beam and a blue NW beam approach the same 2x2 crossing square. In the base
    23	#      task they would redirect or merge depending on the angle. In this combo they keep their diagonal
    24	#      directions, step through the crossing square, and the red continuation is now painted blue while
    25	#      the blue continuation is now painted red. If a later yellow beam crosses the already-drawn blue
    26	#      continuation, that older suffix is recolored yellow-from-the-crossing-onward and the moving beam
    27	#      inherits blue. This realizes the multi-collision color-transfer chain from the combo description.
    28	# b) Complexity calibration:
    29	#    - Pure pass-through is too weak if only one crossing occurs, so generation requires multiple swap
    30	#      events and at least one beam that changes color twice. That keeps the D3-style inheritance chain
    31	#      visible rather than collapsing into a single local exchange.
    32	#    - We still cap each frame to one corner gap, which avoids clutter from many parallel trails and
    33	#      keeps the number of distinct beam colors equal to the number of blocks, a structure humans can
    34	#      track from a few examples.
    35	# c) Implicit-assumption audit against the original main():
    36	#    - The original solver assumed collisions are expressed by geometry changes: turning, zigzagging,
    37	#      truncation, and blank gaps. None of those assumptions survive D2/2, so the combo removes every
    38	#      branch that rewrites direction or blocks a cell on collision.
    39	#    - The original paint logic assumed beam.color is immutable. D3/2 breaks that, so color becomes
    40	#      persistent mutable state attached to each beam and must survive across later collisions.
    41	#    - The original solver could safely forget old beam suffixes after a collision. The combo cannot,
    42	#      because a later crossing with an already-painted segment must recolor the old suffix from the
    43	#      crossing onward to preserve "post-collision uses the inherited color".
    44	#    - Same-cell collisions are ambiguous under a one-layer pass-through drawing model. The combo solver
    45	#      therefore treats them as invalid, and the generator guarantees they never appear by restricting
    46	#      to corner gaps and filtering candidates through the simulator.
    47	# d) Placement legality:
    48	#    - Frames keep the original 4x4 geometry, remain fully in bounds, and preserve the original minimum
    49	#      frame-box clearance so blocks do not overlap.
    50	#    - Candidate corner gaps are accepted only when their diagonals have enough runway through background
    51	#      cells; the simulator then rejects any layout that would create same-cell impacts.
    52	# e) Visual observability:
    53	#    - The geometry alone is now simple: straight diagonal lines. The interesting signal is the sharp
    54	#      color boundary at crossings and the fact that a later crossing can recolor an earlier suffix.
    55	#    - Distinct block colors plus repeated corner-gap emitters give humans a visible way to trace where
    56	#      each continuation color came from without relying on numeric color ids.
    57	# f) Visual cleanliness:
    58	#    - One diagonal beam per frame prevents thick overdraw and keeps each crossing local and legible.
    59	#    - Solver-side rejection of same-cell events removes the main source of unreadable overlap.
    60	# g) Color-change inference:
    61	#    - Every beam color is sourced from a visible 2x2 block, so each post-collision color segment can be
    62	#      traced back to another emitter. Requiring multi-swap samples gives the training set repeated
    63	#      evidence that colors propagate along collision chains instead of being fixed to one beam forever.
    64	# New solver assumptions to review:
    65	#    - Legal combo inputs use structural corner gaps only, so every beam direction is diagonal.
    66	#    - All collisions are geometric diagonal crossings, either simultaneous beam-beam crossings or a
    67	#      later beam crossing an already-painted diagonal segment. The generator enforces both assumptions.
